[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     "attribute_values",
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     "syntactic_clues",
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     "reasoning",
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     "solution"
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Do NOT add any other keys.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m NORMALIZATION RULES
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Use underscores instead of spaces in VALUES (e.g., grilled_cheese, very_short).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Attribute names MUST match the solution_header exactly (case-sensitive), e.g., Name, Animal, Occupation, Sport, Height, etc.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - House numbers are integers 1..N.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Convert ordinals to integers: first=1, second=2, third=3, fourth=4, fifth=5, sixth=6, etc.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Do not invent values. Every value must be mapped to its canonical token (in attribute_values) and selected from the list of allowed attribute_values (after normalization).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m  - Example: If puzzle says “september” and attribute_values contains "sept", output "sept" (not september).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m  - Example: If puzzle says “sept” and attribute_values contains "september", output "september"
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - If the clue mentions a bare person name (e.g., "Arnold"), treat it as Name=Arnold.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - If the clue uses a descriptor like "cat lover", "dog owner", "coffee drinker", map it to the matching token in attribute_values.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 1) DOMAIN OUTPUT (MANDATORY)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - "n_houses" MUST be an integer N equal to the number of houses in the puzzle.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m attribute_values immutability rule:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - The "attribute_values" object MUST be returned exactly as provided in the input.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - It must be identical:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   - Same attribute keys
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   - Same ordering of keys
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   - Same ordering of values within each list
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   - Same casing and spelling
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Do NOT normalize, rename, reorder, add, or remove anything in "attribute_values".
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Normalization rules apply ONLY to syntactic_clues, reasoning, and solution — NOT to attribute_values.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 2) syntactic_clues (MANDATORY, TEXTUAL CONSTRAINTS — NOT PREDICATES)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m We do NOT use predicate-style DSL for clues.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m Instead, each clue MUST be rewritten as a single-line *syntactic constraint statement* in a Z3-like textual form.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m Rules:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - "syntactic_clues" MUST be a list of strings.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - For each clue, the selected tokens must be mapped to one of the values defined in attribute_values.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m  - Example: If the clue says “sept” and attribute_values contains "september", use "september"; if attribute_values contains "sept", use "sept".
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - There MUST be exactly one entry per clue, in the same order as the clues.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Each entry MUST be exactly 1 line and end with a period.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Each entry MUST start with the clue id prefix: "C<i>: ".
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Use ONLY these syntactic operators in the clue text:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     ==   (same house / equivalence)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m    !=   (not same house)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     <    (somewhere left of)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     >    (somewhere right of)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     + k == (k is a positive integer, e.g., 1 for immediately left, 2 for one house between, 3 for two houses between)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     == H  (fixed house index, where H is an integer)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Use bare normalized tokens (no quotes) for values (e.g., Arnold, engineer, very_short).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states a specific house like "in the fifth house", encode as: <token> == 5
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "The lawyer is in the fifth house." -> "C9: lawyer == 5."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "directly left of", encode as: A + 1 == B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "baseball is directly left of engineer" -> "C12: baseball + 1 == engineer."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "one house between", encode as: A + 2 == B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "There is one house between Eric and the bird keeper" -> "C12: Eric + 2 == bird_keeper."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "There is one house between Arnold and Peter" -> "C12: Arnold + 2 == Peter."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "two houses between", encode as: A + 3 == B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "There are two houses between Eric and Arnold" -> "C12: Eric + 3 == Arnold."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "person who has", encode as: A == B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "The person whose mother's name is Holly is the person who has black hair" -> "C12: Holly == black."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "one house between the person who has", encode as: A + 2 == B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "There is one house between the person who has black hair and Eric" -> "C12: black + 2 == Eric."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "next to each other", encode it as: Or(A == B + 1, A == B - 1)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Example: "The person who prefers city breaks and Alice are next to each other" -> C12: "Or(city_breaks == Alice + 1, city_breaks == Alice - 1)."
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "somewhere to the left of", encode as: A < B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "somewhere to the right of", encode as: A > B
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - When a clue states "X is the Y", encode as: X == Y
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m IMPORTANT:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - The goal is to produce constraints that resemble:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   s.add(<left> <op> <right>)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   but you must NOT write "s.add(...)".
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Only output the inner constraint as text.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 3) reasoning (MANDATORY — INTERLEAVED NATURAL + SYNTACTIC)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m ================================================================================
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - "reasoning" MUST be a list of strings.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Each entry MUST be exactly 1 sentence and end with a period.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Reasoning MUST be interleaved:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     Odd-numbered entries: Natural-language reasoning.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     Even-numbered entries: Syntactic reasoning step (Z3-like statement).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Natural-language entries should explain the deduction in plain English.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Syntactic entries should encode the *newly deduced fact* as a Z3-like statement.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Tokens in Syntactic entries should encode the *mapped* to values in "attribute_values".
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m Syntactic entry format:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Every syntactic entry MUST start with "S<k>: " and MUST end with a period.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - <k> starts at 1 and increments by 1 for each syntactic step only (S1, S2, S3,...).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - The syntactic constraint MUST be solver-verifiable and may use ONLY:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   ==,!=, <, >, + d ==, Not(...), And(...), Or(...)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m - Each syntactic step MUST be written in the exact form: S<k>
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Atomic operators:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     ==        (same house / equivalence)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m    !=        (not the same house)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     <         (somewhere to the left of)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     >         (somewhere to the right of)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     + d ==    (directed distance; d is a positive integer)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     == H      (fixed house index; H is an integer in 1..n_houses)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m   Boolean operators:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     Not(e)    (negation of a single atomic expression)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     And(e1, e2,..., en)
[36m(TaskRunner pid=344394)[0m     Or(e1, e2,..., en)
